@meta
id: 02-12
title: The swap file after a crash
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: write, quit
par: 97
minutes: 6
vscode: the "restore unsaved changes?" prompt on restart
open: crash-recovery.md

@brief
The first time vim greets you with a wall of text starting `E325: ATTENTION`
you will assume you have broken something. You have not. While you edit,
vim keeps a hidden file beside yours — `.order.provider.ts.swp` for
`order.provider.ts` — holding every change you have not written yet. If
vim dies, or the ssh session drops, or the laptop sleeps badly, that file
outlives the crash.

The warning means one of two things. Either the editor died and your
unsaved work is sitting there waiting, or the file is genuinely open in
another window right now. The choices vim offers you are the same either
way, and the two you need are `R` to recover and `D` to delete the swap.

The team runbook for this is half written. Add the commands to the three
steps that are missing them, using exactly the text given here:

    3. Compare it with what is on disk: :w recovered.md then diff outside vim
    4. If the recovered buffer is the one you want, write it: :w
    5. Delete the swap file or vim asks again every time: :!rm .orders.md.swp

Everything else in the file stays as it is. Save when the three lines are
complete.

@keys
| `:recover` | load the swap file for the current file by hand |
| `:!cmd` | run a shell command without leaving vim |
| `:w name` | write the buffer elsewhere so you can compare the two |
| `:e!` | reread the file from disk, abandoning the buffer |

@hints
Three lines, three appends. Each addition goes at the end of an existing
line, so `$a` and type.
--
The lines are next to each other, so after each `Esc` a single `j` puts
you on the next one.
--
The colons and exclamation marks are part of the text you are typing into
a markdown file. Nothing here is executed.

@solution
    jjjjjjjjjj $ a  :w recovered.md then diff outside vim <Esc>
    j $ a  :w <Esc>
    j $ a  :!rm .orders.md.swp <Esc>
    ZZ

The recovery itself, when it happens to you for real, is four moves.
Open the file. Press `R` at the prompt. Look at the buffer — it is the
unsaved version, and it may be newer than anything on disk, so check it
rather than trusting it. Write it if it is right. Then delete the swap
file, because vim will keep warning you until it is gone.

Two things that save arguments later. A swap warning on a file a colleague
has open right now is not a crash — recovering it and writing would stamp
on their work, so quit and go and ask them. And a swap file that is
*older* than the file on disk usually means someone else already saved the
real fix; recovering it takes you backwards.

The swap file is a crash net, not an undo history. It holds the current
unwritten state of the buffer, not the path you took to get there. Undo
history is a separate thing, and by default it dies with the process —
which is the subject of the next lesson.

@start crash-recovery.md
# Recovering from a vim crash

Vim keeps a swap file next to the file you are editing. It holds every
change you have made and not yet written. It survives a crash, and the
next time you open the file vim shows E325 and offers you the choices.

## Steps

1. Read the message. Note the process id and whether it still runs.
2. Press R at the prompt to recover the buffer from the swap file.
3. Compare it with what is on disk:
4. If the recovered buffer is the one you want, write it:
5. Delete the swap file or vim asks again every time:

